// pfodWeb NOTE: excludes GPIO1/2 (CAN), GPIO8/17/18 (RS485), GPIO9-15
// (RTC INT + W5500 Ethernet SPI bus), GPIO16/21/47/48 (A7670G cellular
// modem) - see vendor pins_arduino.h. Independently re-verified 2026-07-14
// against the official Seeed Studio wiki
// (https://wiki.seeedstudio.com/Edgebox-ESP-100-Arduino/), which publishes
// the exact GPIO table: CAN_TXD/RXD IO1/IO2, RS485 TXD/RXD/RTS IO17/IO18/
// IO8, Ethernet(W5500) CS0/MISO/MOSI/SCK/INT/RST IO10/IO11/IO12/IO13/IO14/
// IO15, cellular modem(A7670G) PWR_EN/PWR_KEY/TXD/RXD IO16/IO21/IO48/IO47,
// RTC interrupt IO9, I2C SCL/SDA IO19/IO20 - every exclusion above matches
// exactly, confirmed correct.
// pfodWeb NOTE (correction, 2026-07-14): GPIO0 tagged "button" - the
// official Seeed Studio wiki (link above) and the independent
// espboards.dev pinout aggregator (https://www.espboards.dev/esp32/edgebox-esp-100/)
// both explicitly identify GPIO0 as wired to a physical onboard "Reset
// Button" (not merely the universal ESP32 BOOT/download-mode strapping
// pin present on every board) - capabilities changed from
// [digital_input, digital_output, pwm_output] to [digital_input, button],
// matching this repo's established button-tag convention (e.g.
// crabik_slot_esp32_s3's GPIO0).
// pfodWeb NOTE (correction, 2026-07-14): GPIO35/36/37 were missing from
// this board's chipGpios (silently inherited the chip-level default's
// octal-PSRAM exclusion for GPIO33-37), but the Seeed Studio wiki (link
// above) and espboards.dev both confirm these are this board's real,
// wired DO5/DO4/DO3 industrial digital-output terminals (matching this
// board's own pins_arduino.h: DO5=35, DO4=36, DO3=37) - the vendor could
// not have wired these to external terminal-block outputs if they were
// truly reserved for octal PSRAM, so this board's ESP32-S3 module
// evidently does not use octal PSRAM. Restored as plain digital I/O
// (matching DO0-DO2 on GPIO40/39/38, already present), since the
// board's own header comment already states "DO0-5/DI0-3/AO0-1 stay
// kept - the industrial I/O terminal banks are the point of using this
// board."
